67
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:44,640
I wonder how much more of this I can watch.

68
00:03:44,760 --> 00:03:46,860
Okay. Okay. All right, see you there.

69
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:47,540
See you later.

70
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:48,140
Good. Bye -bye.

71
00:03:48,240 --> 00:03:56,420
Bye. Yes! No, no, no.

72
00:03:56,480 --> 00:04:00,280
Mrs. Kerwin, you see, we suspect that Easttown is giving you and your neighbors a

73
00:04:00,280 --> 00:04:02,560
lot of false information about what's been dumped in that reservoir.

74
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:05,420
Right. Uh -huh.

75
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:09,720
Uh -huh. See, that's just what I mean.

76
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:12,460
You see, there's no such thing as chunky style water.

77
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:15,440
Uh -huh. For the record?

78
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:16,600
Good. Let's see.

79
00:04:17,360 --> 00:04:18,960
Oh, just one minute, okay?

80
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:21,240
Uh -huh. How about Monday?

81
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:24,380
There we go.

82
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:27,200
Good. Okay. Mind if I make a call?

83
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:28,160
Sure. Right over there.

84
00:04:29,020 --> 00:04:31,360
Uh -huh. Okay, what is this?

85
00:04:33,860 --> 00:04:36,540
I want to place a call, not launch an MX missile.

86
00:04:47,740 --> 00:04:48,900
Hello, this is Dr.

87
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:50,460
Seaver. Mr. Anderson, in, please.

88
00:04:51,940 --> 00:04:52,920
Oh, no, I'm sorry.

89
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:54,380
I must have misdialed then.

90
00:04:54,460 --> 00:04:57,920
I was... No, I don't wish to place a classified ad.

91
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:02,340
Now, I'm sure that's a very good per -word price.

92
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:04,240
But, no, I was...

93
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:06,080
I'm trying to get an outside line here.

94
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:10,800
No, I'm not gonna be placing the ad with anyone else.

95
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:12,440
I'm trying to reach a patient.

96
00:05:15,780 --> 00:05:19,760
Mildly schizophrenic, delusions of grandeur, occasional...

97
00:05:19,760 --> 00:05:20,940
Let's go with this one.

98
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:22,940
Yes, yes, I'll be right with you, Mrs.

99
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:24,460
Kerwick. Uh -huh, uh -huh.

100
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:25,460
And this one.

101
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:36,100
Maggie! I think I just leveled Moscow.

102
00:05:37,460 --> 00:05:38,840
Okay, I'm all set here.

103
00:05:39,300 --> 00:05:40,420
So where do you want to eat?

104
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:42,060
Hey, Max, want to grab some lunch?

105
00:05:43,860 --> 00:05:46,540
Fred, I want you to meet my husband, Jason Seabrook.

106
00:05:47,660 --> 00:05:49,320
Oh, jeez, I didn't even see you down there.

107
00:05:51,900 --> 00:05:53,200
Jason, this is Fred Mathis.

108
00:05:53,300 --> 00:05:55,280
Fred's the one I'm working with on the Toxic Waste Store.

109
00:05:55,980 --> 00:05:57,260
Oh. No, no, don't get up.

110
00:05:57,700 --> 00:05:58,920
Hey, it's really nice to meet you.

111
00:05:58,940 --> 00:05:59,960
Well, it's nice to meet you.

112
00:05:59,980 --> 00:06:01,140
Mags told me a lot about you.

113
00:06:02,700 --> 00:06:05,080
Oh. Well, why didn't you tell me about Fred?

114
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:07,240
Well, poor Mags has stuck with me all day.

115
00:06:07,300 --> 00:06:09,780
I'm sure the last thing she wants to do when she gets home is talk about me.

116
00:06:10,020 --> 00:06:11,220
Oh, that's not true.

117
00:06:11,220 --> 00:06:14,140
Well, I told you about Fred just yesterday, don't you remember?

118
00:06:16,060 --> 00:06:20,360
Oh, yeah! Well, I think I just pictured you differently.

119
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:23,700
Bald or... Badder.

120
00:06:24,420 --> 00:06:25,600
Well, that's the public for you.

121
00:06:25,680 --> 00:06:27,060
They think we all look like Lou Grant.

122
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:29,200
Thank God Mags doesn't, huh, Jason?

123
00:06:29,420 --> 00:06:33,060
Mm. Uh, Jason and I were just about to grab a bite.

124
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:33,880
Well, I won't keep you.

125
00:06:33,880 --> 00:06:38,060
Excuse me. Oh, uh, Mags, I will need to see you for a couple of minutes before that

126
00:06:38,060 --> 00:06:38,800
meeting at 2 o 'clock.

127
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:41,980
And it's... Oh.

128
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:45,220
Well, look, Fred, why don't you just join us for lunch?

129
00:06:45,220 --> 00:06:45,680
I don't want to.

130
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:46,520
No, no. I insist.

131
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:47,860
You two have work to do together?

132
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:49,360
That's very nice of you, Jason.

133
00:06:50,020 --> 00:06:51,020
Mags, I didn't tell you.

134
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:54,480
Two of these goons tried to muscle me when I was out of that Easttown dump.

135
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:55,920
Must have been some big thing.

136
00:06:55,920 --> 00:06:57,380
I believe you.

137
00:06:58,120 --> 00:06:58,980
Come on, Carol.

138
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:00,780
Why can't you just write this essay for me?

139
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:02,360
Because it's your assignment.

140
00:07:02,700 --> 00:07:04,160
Yeah, but you'd do a better job.

141
00:07:04,240 --> 00:07:07,940
I mean, look, you're standing on principle, and the result is that the essay

142
00:07:07,940 --> 00:07:10,760
suffers. Now, do you really want that on your conscience?

143
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:13,560
That's the private hell I choose to live within.

144
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:16,740
You what? Maggie?

145
00:07:18,240 --> 00:07:19,500
Thought I heard a car door slam.

146
00:07:19,960 --> 00:07:24,020
No, you know, Dad, no matter how many times I ask the neighbors to please climb out

147
00:07:24,020 --> 00:07:26,160
through their windows, they insist on using those doors.

148
00:07:27,740 --> 00:07:29,580
Don't be a wise guy, Mike.

149
00:07:30,380 --> 00:07:31,520
Carol, what time's your watch say?

150
00:07:32,060 --> 00:07:33,320
Um, 10 -03.

151
00:07:33,940 --> 00:07:34,940
Why? Is yours broken?

152
00:07:35,360 --> 00:07:37,360
No, no, it says 10 -02.

153
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:39,500
Just thought it might have stopped.

154
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:43,160
Apparently it did, Dad, but it sounds like you've nipped it in the bud.

155
00:07:49,220 --> 00:07:54,240
Yeah. Fred. Ah, yeah.

156
00:07:54,260 --> 00:07:55,840
No, I'll have her call you right away.

157
00:07:57,500 --> 00:08:00,400
Okay. You too.

158
00:08:01,700 --> 00:08:05,340
Yeah. Bye. Fred?

159
00:08:06,020 --> 00:08:09,480
Yeah, Fred's the guy your mother's working on that toxic waste story with.

160
00:08:09,480 --> 00:08:11,280
Seems like a nice guy.

161
00:08:12,140 --> 00:08:14,900
Very nice. Darn nice.

162
00:08:17,720 --> 00:08:22,640
Whoo! Will you guys act your age?

163
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:28,160
Whoo! Hi, everybody.

164
00:08:28,420 --> 00:08:31,580
Hey, Mom. I didn't even hear your car door slam.

165
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:35,380
Well, uh, I'll try and do it louder next time.

166
00:08:36,580 --> 00:08:37,820
Well, guys, how's it going?

167
00:08:38,060 --> 00:08:38,920
What'd you do today?

168
00:08:39,460 --> 00:08:40,540
Well, I went to...

169
00:08:40,540 --> 00:08:41,640
Nothing. They're children.

170
00:08:42,360 --> 00:08:44,420
They have boring, uneventful lives.

171
00:08:44,980 --> 00:08:45,740
How was your day?

172
00:08:45,780 --> 00:08:46,740
Tell us about that story.

173
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:50,800
Oh, Jason, I'm sorry our lunch got turned into a work session.

174
00:08:51,240 --> 00:08:52,060
Oh, no, are you kidding?

175
00:08:52,140 --> 00:08:53,220
That was great for me.

176
00:08:53,720 --> 00:08:57,200
It was shocking for me to discover how little I knew about sludge.

177
00:08:58,940 --> 00:09:00,160
What about the Easttown dump?

178
00:09:00,780 --> 00:09:02,900
Oh, honey, I am really bushed.

179
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:05,920
I'd rather not even think about that stuff for a while, okay?

180
00:09:07,180 --> 00:09:11,560
Okay. Sure. Hey, Dad, don't forget to tell her Fred just called.

181
00:09:11,760 --> 00:09:13,880
Fred called? Oh, good, I need to talk to him.

182
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:15,340
We're gonna break the story tomorrow.

183
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:18,760
If it means working till midnight, oh, I just can't wait to nail those creeps.

184
00:09:20,500 --> 00:09:23,560
Fred? Hi. Ah!

185
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:24,960
You're kidding me!

186
00:09:25,280 --> 00:09:37,600
Oh! So how did everything go?

187
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:41,180
Did everyone enjoy my first foray into the wonderful world of pancakes?

188
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:45,640
Well, it beat the heck out of your foray into the wonderful world of liver.

189
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:51,900
Dad, did they have science projects when you were in third grade?

190
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:55,200
Ben, when Dad was in the third grade, they didn't have science.

191
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:00,700
In fact, when Dad was in the third grade, they didn't have the third grade.

192
00:10:01,600 --> 00:10:02,380
Ha -ha -ha.

193
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:04,040
Ha -ha -ha -ha.

194
00:10:04,400 --> 00:10:08,540
Uh, Mike, they say the sense of humor is the final stage of child development.

195
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:11,360
You ought to feel it coming on any time now.

196
00:10:13,900 --> 00:10:16,600
Yes, Ben, I did a wonderful project in third grade.

197
00:10:17,100 --> 00:10:18,500
I grew mold on bread.

198
00:10:18,840 --> 00:10:26,660
On purpose? Yes, yes, I determined that mold would grow faster on white bread than

199
00:10:26,660 --> 00:10:31,180
on pumpernickel. I think I should strike out new directions.

200
00:10:32,700 --> 00:10:36,420
Well, what you really have to do is find something that interests you, and that way,

201
00:10:36,460 --> 00:10:38,040
the project will teach you the most.

202
00:10:39,120 --> 00:10:40,820
Frank's here. Hi, Mom.

203
00:10:40,880 --> 00:10:41,920
Bye, sweetie. Have a good day.

204
00:10:42,100 --> 00:10:43,040
Thanks talking to you, Mom.

205
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:44,220
You look great again.

206
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:45,900
Thanks. Looking good yourself.

207
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:48,180
Well, yeah, you know, I've been cutting down on the beer.

208
00:10:49,280 --> 00:10:50,520
Me too. Me too.

209
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:51,320
Have a good day.

210
00:10:53,660 --> 00:10:54,780
I'm out of here, too.

211
00:10:55,060 --> 00:10:57,400
Oh, remember, Fred and I will probably be working late again tonight.

212
00:10:58,080 --> 00:11:00,400
Okay. So what time do you think you'll be home?

213
00:11:00,860 --> 00:11:01,840
Well, it's hard to...

214
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:04,180
Estimate. Oh, I don't know.

215
00:11:04,180 --> 00:11:06,380
between nine and 11, I'd say.

216
00:11:06,540 --> 00:11:07,780
You call that an estimate?

217
00:11:08,160 --> 00:11:10,700
No, an estimate is 905, 906.

218
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:15,720
Okay, okay, let's say between 10 and 1015.

219
00:11:17,340 --> 00:11:19,020
Well, which one, 10 or 1015?

220
00:11:20,400 --> 00:11:23,280
I don't know, Jason, it's only 15 minutes.

221
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:24,940
Well, it's only 15 minutes.

222
00:11:25,560 --> 00:11:27,540
Maggie, a lot can happen in 15 minutes.

223
00:11:28,440 --> 00:11:30,940
The Titanic sunk in 15 minutes.

224
00:11:32,040 --> 00:11:35,320
Charlton Heston picked up 10 Commandments in 15 minutes.

225
00:11:35,940 --> 00:11:39,120
Our first child was conceived in 15 minutes.

226
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:47,500
Tops. Jason. Are you jealous?

227
00:11:48,780 --> 00:11:49,900
Jealous? Uh -huh.

228
00:11:50,100 --> 00:11:54,980
Of what? Of me and Fred, you know, working late.

229
00:11:55,020 --> 00:11:56,100
Oh, of you and, uh...

230
00:11:56,100 --> 00:11:58,180
Uh -huh. That is a comical thought.

231
00:12:02,520 --> 00:12:04,960
Jason, you are jealous.

232
00:12:05,340 --> 00:12:06,980
I'm not. Oh, come on.

233
00:12:07,120 --> 00:12:07,280
Yes, you are.

234
00:12:07,300 --> 00:12:08,320
No, I'm not jealous, Maggie.

235
00:12:08,520 --> 00:12:09,540
I'm a psychiatrist.

236
00:12:09,720 --> 00:12:12,920
I'm also a man with a heightened degree of self -awareness.

237
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:17,120
And for that reason, I can admit to a certain sense of loss over the fact that you

238
00:12:17,120 --> 00:12:22,700
now share things with a professional colleague until 10 or 11 at night, things you

239
00:12:22,700 --> 00:12:24,080
and I are unable to share.

240
00:12:24,240 --> 00:12:27,900
Uh -huh. But I hasten to add, I would feel exactly the same way if your colleague

241
00:12:27,900 --> 00:12:28,380
were a woman.

242
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:30,800
Oh. Well, you're probably right.

243
00:12:30,920 --> 00:12:32,520
Well, there's no probably about it, Maggie.

244
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:36,300
Analyzing people's motivations is what I do for a living.

245
00:12:36,500 --> 00:12:38,160
I am not sexually jealous.

246
00:12:41,420 --> 00:12:45,080
I have far too much faith in you to ever be sexually jealous.

247
00:12:45,080 --> 00:12:47,760
And I would appreciate it if you had the same faith in me.

248
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:53,300
I'm sorry, Jason.

249
00:12:53,300 --> 00:12:54,540
I was just kidding.

250
00:12:54,820 --> 00:12:57,000
Mm -hmm. Forgive me?

251
00:12:59,140 --> 00:13:00,960
Well, okay. Okay.

252
00:13:01,800 --> 00:13:02,980
Good. I'll see you later.

253
00:13:04,020 --> 00:13:05,380
So what time you think you'll be home?

254
00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:23,340
Mike, cut it out.

255
00:13:24,100 --> 00:13:25,380
What? I wasn't doing anything.

256
00:13:25,380 --> 00:13:26,780
Will you stop bothering your sister?

257
00:13:34,480 --> 00:13:36,360
Hey, Dad, what's another word for election?

258
00:13:36,660 --> 00:13:39,880
Plobocyte. I didn't ask you, I asked Dad.

259
00:13:40,440 --> 00:13:41,500
What's the difference?

260
00:13:42,080 --> 00:13:44,440
The difference is I don't wanna know from you.

261
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:53,300
Sorry. How do you spell it?

262
00:13:55,620 --> 00:13:57,800
Why should I tell you how to spell it?

263
00:13:58,480 --> 00:14:00,060
Because I asked you.

264
00:14:00,560 --> 00:14:02,560
I thought you didn't want to know things from me.

265
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:03,980
What are you, a lawyer?

266
00:14:04,440 --> 00:14:08,700
I mean, I'm trying to do you a favor here by finding a use for your brain, and out

267
00:14:08,700 --> 00:14:10,740
of nowhere you turn on me like a wild dog.

268
00:14:22,820 --> 00:14:24,180
Cut it out.

269
00:14:25,640 --> 00:14:31,080
Female subject grabs wrist after third click.

270
00:14:35,880 --> 00:14:38,420
You do that again and I'll kill you.

271
00:14:41,700 --> 00:14:45,780
Male subject threatens death after first click.

272
00:14:49,680 --> 00:14:51,280
Ben, what are you doing?

273
00:14:51,580 --> 00:14:53,200
It's my science project.

274
00:14:53,200 --> 00:14:57,820
I'm testing to see how long it takes certain people to respond to certain stimuli.

275
00:14:58,860 --> 00:15:01,760
Well, that's not a good science project, so stop it.

276
00:15:03,840 --> 00:15:07,020
But you told me to do something that interests me.

277
00:15:07,340 --> 00:15:09,740
Mike and Carol interest me more than mold.

278
00:15:15,780 --> 00:15:17,540
That's a touching sentiment, Ben.

279
00:15:18,380 --> 00:15:19,400
Now cut it out.

280
00:15:21,060 --> 00:15:22,220
What's with him?

281
00:15:23,500 --> 00:15:24,580
He doesn't like you.

282
00:15:24,620 --> 00:15:25,200
He never has.

283
00:15:33,140 --> 00:15:34,500
Hey, Dad, when's Mom coming home?

284
00:15:34,740 --> 00:15:36,580
Do I look like Gene Dixon, Mike?

285
00:15:38,660 --> 00:15:42,660
Your mother's a working adult, and when she's finished working, she'll come home.

286
00:15:43,340 --> 00:15:47,740
I wish she'd get here, because she thinks of great science projects.

287
00:15:48,540 --> 00:15:52,300
She does? Last year she picked tadpoles.

288
00:15:53,280 --> 00:15:54,400
Well, that's it, then.

289
00:15:54,880 --> 00:15:57,060
I can't do tadpoles again.

290
00:15:57,520 --> 00:15:59,280
They keep these things on file.

291
00:16:01,820 --> 00:16:03,040
No, I mean your mother.

292
00:16:03,960 --> 00:16:05,980
We should be asking your mother what to do.

293
00:16:06,340 --> 00:16:09,660
In fact, I think I'll go down to that newspaper office right now and ask her.

294
00:16:10,620 --> 00:16:11,960
Dad, why don't you just call her?

295
00:16:12,620 --> 00:16:15,180
Obviously, you haven't seen the phones they have down there.

296
00:16:15,520 --> 00:16:17,380
Believe me, it'd be a lot simpler this way.

297
00:16:17,380 --> 00:16:19,380
Won't that be bothering her, Dad?

298
00:16:19,920 --> 00:16:21,160
Absolutely not. No, no.

299
00:16:21,160 --> 00:16:23,300
Your mother wants to be involved in these decisions.

300
00:16:23,760 --> 00:16:27,440
Besides, I don't like the idea of her being down at the office this late at night

301
00:16:27,440 --> 00:16:28,480
all by herself.

302
00:16:29,060 --> 00:16:30,520
She's not all by herself.

303
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:32,120
Frank's with her.

304
00:16:34,560 --> 00:17:06,480
Even so. I'm sorry.

305
00:17:06,480 --> 00:17:06,700
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

306
00:17:06,700 --> 00:17:06,940
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

307
00:17:07,420 --> 00:17:10,040
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

308
00:17:10,040 --> 00:17:10,800
I'm sorry. I'm sorry.

309
00:17:10,800 --> 00:17:12,300
from that Easttown dump site.

310
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:13,360
Oh, wait a minute.

311
00:17:13,360 --> 00:17:14,800
I'll call, please.

312
00:17:17,380 --> 00:17:20,940
BELL RINGS Go.

313
00:17:27,700 --> 00:17:29,820
One, two, three...

314
00:17:30,460 --> 00:17:37,300
Ah! Jason. Jason?

315
00:17:37,400 --> 00:17:38,160
Are you all right?

316
00:17:38,460 --> 00:17:41,180
Oh. Honey, sit down.

317
00:17:41,460 --> 00:17:41,980
No, no, I'm fine.

318
00:17:41,980 --> 00:17:43,700
Sit down. Hold on.

319
00:17:44,480 --> 00:17:47,800
Unfortunately, my nose stopped the door before it could hit my face.

320
00:17:48,540 --> 00:17:50,640
Jason, what are you doing here?

321
00:17:51,220 --> 00:17:52,560
What am I doing here?

322
00:17:52,700 --> 00:17:54,760
Uh -huh. It's about Ben.

323
00:17:55,780 --> 00:17:56,660
Jason, what is it?

324
00:17:56,680 --> 00:17:57,360
Is it serious?

325
00:17:57,500 --> 00:17:58,460
Well, of course it's serious.

326
00:17:58,460 --> 00:17:59,940
I wouldn't come down here if it wasn't serious.

327
00:18:00,040 --> 00:18:02,220
Would I? I don't know what to do with the boy, Maggie.

328
00:18:02,260 --> 00:18:04,940
He's got a bad attitude about mold.

329
00:18:05,120 --> 00:18:08,220
And it's just not ethical for him to do tadpoles again.

330
00:18:09,460 --> 00:18:12,400
Excuse me. This sounds personal.

331
00:18:23,720 --> 00:18:26,580
You came down here to check up on me, didn't you?

332
00:18:26,800 --> 00:18:28,640
Oh. Oh, well, that's great.

333
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:29,960
That's just great.

334
00:18:30,140 --> 00:18:35,960
I come down to discuss the welfare of our youngest son, our baby, if you will, And

335
00:18:35,960 --> 00:18:37,900
you twist it into something sordid.

336
00:18:38,520 --> 00:18:39,100
I have a good mind.

337
00:18:39,180 --> 00:18:41,000
I should just turn around and walk out of here right now.

338
00:18:41,240 --> 00:18:42,400
I don't believe this.

339
00:18:42,560 --> 00:18:47,180
I mean, you thought, you actually thought there was something going on between me

340
00:18:47,180 --> 00:18:48,440
and Fred? Oh.

341
00:18:50,620 --> 00:18:53,040
I can't even dignify that with a response.

342
00:18:54,020 --> 00:18:55,360
You like him better than me, don't you?

343
00:18:56,600 --> 00:18:58,200
Please. Come on, Maggie, admit it.

344
00:18:58,200 --> 00:18:59,180
I'm not a complete idiot.

345
00:18:59,460 --> 00:19:00,680
Maggie, do you want some eggs?

346
00:19:00,780 --> 00:19:03,040
No time. Well, Maggie, how about we meet for lunch?

347
00:19:03,100 --> 00:19:04,220
Oh, I completely forgot.

348
00:19:04,220 --> 00:19:06,140
Well, Maggie, how was your day today?

349
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:07,200
I don't want to talk about it.

350
00:19:07,380 --> 00:19:08,160
I'm done. Fred called?

351
00:19:08,220 --> 00:19:09,740
Oh, I have time to talk to him.

352
00:19:10,660 --> 00:19:12,500
Jason. Don't Jason me, Maggie.

353
00:19:12,620 --> 00:19:14,920
I've also been watching the way you're dressing lately.

354
00:19:14,960 --> 00:19:16,840
And now I understand it.

355
00:19:16,860 --> 00:19:20,580
Because when he looks at you, he looks at you as if you were...

356
00:19:20,580 --> 00:19:21,500
As if you were a woman.

357
00:19:23,360 --> 00:19:27,800
Yeah, you know, at first I couldn't figure that out, and then it dawned on me.

358
00:19:28,100 --> 00:19:29,800
I am a woman.

359
00:19:29,980 --> 00:19:31,760
So you admit it.

360
00:19:32,720 --> 00:19:33,920
You're into this, Maggie.

361
00:19:33,960 --> 00:19:34,660
You enjoy it.

362
00:19:34,900 --> 00:19:36,280
Yeah, I enjoy it.

363
00:19:36,380 --> 00:19:37,520
People find me interesting.

364
00:19:37,620 --> 00:19:38,640
That makes me feel good.

365
00:19:38,680 --> 00:19:39,500
What's wrong with that?

366
00:19:39,580 --> 00:19:42,520
Well, what's wrong with it is you're putting your husband through a living hell.

367
00:19:42,700 --> 00:19:45,780
And, Maggie, I would never do that to you.

368
00:19:46,400 --> 00:19:49,560
Ah! What do you mean, ah?

369
00:19:49,980 --> 00:19:54,420
Ah, it means I spent 15 years in sweatpants cleaning toilets while you went down to

370
00:19:54,420 --> 00:19:58,780
your office in your sexy psychiatrist sweater and your sexy psychiatrist jacket.

371
00:19:58,860 --> 00:20:00,360
My jackets aren't sexy, Maggie.

372
00:20:00,360 --> 00:20:02,640
They're tweed. Women die for Tweed and you don't.

373
00:20:03,520 --> 00:20:05,060
I have no control over that.

374
00:20:05,240 --> 00:20:08,600
And how many nights did I spend watching your broccoli go limp while I waited for

375
00:20:08,600 --> 00:20:09,240
you to come home?

376
00:20:09,400 --> 00:20:11,000
I was fighting traffic, Maggie.

377
00:20:11,160 --> 00:20:14,140
Yeah, with that brilliant young psychiatrist in your carpool.

378
00:20:15,220 --> 00:20:17,760
Dr. Rosenblum? Dr.

379
00:20:17,760 --> 00:20:21,780
Jennifer Rosenblum, who, as I noticed, always managed to ride in the front seat next

380
00:20:21,780 --> 00:20:24,760
to you. Maggie, she had very long legs.

381
00:20:28,980 --> 00:20:30,820
Well, that's a relief.

382
00:20:30,940 --> 00:20:34,840
I hope leg room wasn't a problem when the two of you jetted off to Chicago for that

383
00:20:34,840 --> 00:20:37,740
psychiatric convention. We were writing a paper together.

384
00:20:37,860 --> 00:20:38,820
Yes, I remember it.

385
00:20:38,820 --> 00:20:40,240
Human sexual inhibitions.

386
00:20:40,380 --> 00:20:41,440
Use them or lose them.

387
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:45,240
Maggie, that was strictly a professional relationship.

388
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,360
What do you think this is?

389
00:20:59,260 --> 00:21:01,080
Was that a rhetorical question?

390
00:21:05,760 --> 00:21:09,000
Waiting for someone else to come home all the time is hard, Jason.

391
00:21:10,380 --> 00:21:12,300
But you get better at it.

392
00:21:13,220 --> 00:21:14,120
I hope so.

393
00:21:14,120 --> 00:21:24,420
I'm sorry. Suddenly I feel very silly.

394
00:21:25,400 --> 00:21:27,240
Well, silly's a strong word.

395
00:21:27,720 --> 00:21:30,580
Not entirely inappropriate, but strong.

396
00:21:36,100 --> 00:21:38,320
Jason... I love you.

397
00:21:38,340 --> 00:21:40,700
I could never cheat on you.

398
00:21:41,540 --> 00:21:51,500
Yeah? Yeah. Well, thank you.

399
00:21:51,680 --> 00:21:58,680
You're welcome. Caterpillars!

400
00:22:02,520 --> 00:22:06,180
What? It's obvious Ben should do caterpillars.

401
00:22:07,020 --> 00:22:08,620
Oh, now that's a great idea.

402
00:22:08,740 --> 00:22:11,760
Well, thanks. It's a good thing I came down here tonight.

403
00:22:11,760 --> 00:22:20,680
Oh. Oh. Okay.

404
00:22:27,080 --> 00:22:32,240
Okay. So how's Ben doing with his caterpillars?

405
00:22:32,580 --> 00:22:34,780
Somebody else was already doing caterpillars.

406
00:22:35,220 --> 00:22:36,780
Ben had to think of a new project.

407
00:22:37,340 --> 00:22:39,880
Oh. A new project?

408
00:22:40,640 --> 00:22:45,060
Please. Pretty good.

409
00:22:47,040 --> 00:23:00,420
Sam. Hey, where's my mold experiment?

410
00:23:11,880 --> 00:23:13,540
LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE
